# Chroma content pack Politics: THRESHOLD SEASONS for the big rungs (moments 72 to 91 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, Part 5). Five
# short seasons of four linked moments each, for the first months after a person takes local councillor, mayor, member
# of parliament, minister or head of government. The crossing life events of politics-events.lib ('polling day in the
# ward', 'the town needs a mayor', 'election night', 'the phone call from the leader', 'the country goes to the polls')
# are the rites that open them; any other way into the title opens the season too. Emren's line is the heart of it:
# winning office changes duties and relationships rather than ending the road, so every season asks whom the person
# now answers to (the ward, the party, the cause, the day job, the family, the friends who now want favours).
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py, plus the season fields the engine accepted (chroma-packs/for-the-engine.md,
# "Engine answer"): threshold: title:<name>, step: 1 | 2 | 3 (the engine brings them about 1, 10 and 22 weeks after the
# title is gained, inside a season of about 26 to 30 weeks, one moment per step), transform: yes (the season's
# transforming chance). Every moment: tier inner, alpha even, stakes .8, holds: the title, the five one-color options
# and one full cross cycle; each season uses the cycles s1 to s4 once. Worlds (Emren, 21:37): every moment has earth:,
# tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of worlds.py, an untagged tribal and magic scene, and option wording where the
# act reads differently there. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md. Pathways and packs thread, 2026-10-05.

# ================================================================ local councillor (ages 18-90)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 72. step 1 (cycle s1)

== the first full council meeting
tier: inner
threshold: title:local councillor
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
times: per new local councillor: once, in the first weeks after taking the title; about 1 life in 100 (catalogue share)
holds: local councillor
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: public life, community
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, elder
earth: the first full meeting of the council: an agenda of two hundred pages, a seat on the back benches and a vote on the first night
tribal: the first council fire at which you sit among the voices, and the elders watch to see whom you look to before you speak
magic: the first sitting of the town council in the guild hall: a carved bench, a sealed roll of business and a vote before the evening bell
requires: in the threshold season of local councillor, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'polling day in the ward' or any other way in): step 1, the first weeks in the seat
likelier: a first seat, won at the last election; a council where the person's side has just come to power; a full agenda in the first month
rarer: a seat taken by someone who served the council for years as an officer or a clerk; a small parish council that meets a few times a year
scene: The agenda for {Ns} first full council meeting runs to two hundred pages, and the vote on the bins is the first item. {N} sits in the back row of the chamber behind a name card, and {boss} leans over to say which way the group is voting.
scene W: {N} has read the standing orders twice and knows when a member may speak, how to second a motion and what a point of order is. It feels like learning the rules of a new house.
scene U: {N} has tabbed every report and found two figures in the budget papers that do not add up.
scene B: {N} watches whom the officers defer to, who sits beside the leader, and which committee chairs are still unfilled.
scene R: {N} wants to stand up on the first night and say what the ward sent {N} here to say, procedure or not.
scene G: {N} sits in the seat that a councillor from the same streets held for twenty years, and feels the weight of everyone who sat there before.
scene tribal: {N} sits for the first time among the voices at the council fire, close enough to feel its heat. The elders watch to see whom {N} looks to before speaking.
scene magic: In the guild hall {N} takes a carved bench for the first time, and the clerk unrolls the evening's business under the warded lamps.
- W | learn the standing orders by heart before speaking once in the chamber | v: conformity, security | mark: learned a skill | tribal: learn the customs of the council fire by heart before speaking once | magic: learn the council's rules and rites by heart before speaking once | chance: 90
- U | read every report, and ask the officers about the two figures that do not add up | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: hear every family's account, and ask the elders about the two that do not agree | magic: read every roll, and ask the clerks about the two sums that do not add up | chance: 84
- B | ask the leader for a seat on the planning committee in the first week | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask the head of the faction for a place among those who choose the next camp | magic: ask the faction's lord for a seat on the council's building committee | chance: 40
- R | stand up on the first night and speak about the ward's worst street, unscripted | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: speak at the first fire about the hearths that flood every spring, as the words come | magic: rise at the first sitting and speak about the ward's worst lane, unscripted | chance: 75
- G | ask the longest-serving councillor how things are really done here | v: tradition | mark: made a friend | tribal: ask the oldest voice at the fire how things are really decided | chance: 84
- W>U | sign up for every training session the council runs for new members | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: sit with the elders each evening to learn the old judgments | magic: attend every lesson the council's clerks give new members | chance: 95
- U>B | map who chairs what, who votes with whom and where the deals are made | v: power, achievement | tribal: work out who speaks for whom, which families stand together and where bargains are struck | chance: 75
- B>R | vote against your own group on the first night, to show your vote is your own | v: self-direction, power | mark: defied an authority | tribal: stand against your own faction at the first fire, to show your voice is your own | magic: cast against your own faction at the first sitting, to show your vote is your own | chance: 75
- R>G | bring a few neighbours from the ward to sit in the public gallery | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: bring families from your own hearths to sit at the edge of the firelight | magic: bring neighbours from the ward to the hall's public benches | chance: 94
- G>W | report back after every meeting in the community hall, as your predecessor always did | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: go back to your own hearths after every fire and tell them what was said | magic: report back after every sitting in the ward's tavern, as the old member always did | chance: 86
works: By the end of the night {N} knows where to sit, when to speak and whom to ask.
works: {boss} catches {N} on the way out and says it was a good first meeting.
fails: The meeting runs late into the night, and {N} goes home having understood half of it.
fails: Something {N} said or did on the first night is all anyone talks about the next morning, and not kindly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 73. step 2 (cycle s2)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 3 options (check.py section 4).
== the casework that never stops
tier: inner
threshold: title:local councillor
step: 2
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
times: per new local councillor: once, about ten weeks into the threshold season, for about half of new councillors (the season's middle step brings this moment or 'whose councillor you are'); about 1 life in 100 sits on a council (catalogue share), so about 1 in 200 meet it
holds: local councillor
stakes: .8
tone: trouble
life: public life, family
horizon: months
roles: partner, colleague, boss
earth: ten weeks in, the emails, the calls and the knocks at the door have not stopped, and the day job and the family are paying for it
tribal: two moons in, the families bring their quarrels to your hearth morning and night, and your own kin eat alone
magic: ten weeks in, petitions come under the door every day, sealed and unsealed, and your own trade waits
requires: in the threshold season of local councillor, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, some weeks into the seat, once residents have learned where the councillor lives
likelier: a ward with poor housing or a failing service; a councillor who is known in the streets; a council office that answers slowly
rarer: a quiet ward; a councillor with a paid assistant or a seat on a council that handles casework centrally
scene: Ten weeks in, {N} has two hundred unanswered messages, a phone that rings at dinner and a neighbour who stops {N} in the shop about a blocked drain. {partner} has started to say "the council" the way people say the name of a rival.
scene W: Every case deserves an answer, in order, within a week. {N} keeps a list, and it is getting longer, not shorter.
scene U: {N} notices that half the cases are the same few problems: the same landlord, the same bus stop, the same broken system at the housing office.
scene B: Some residents vote and some do not; some cases make a name and some vanish. {N} is ashamed to have noticed, and has noticed anyway.
scene R: A mother in tears on the doorstep about her damp flat makes {N} so angry that the rest of the list can wait.
scene G: These are {Ns} own streets. The woman with the drain taught {N} to swim as a child.
scene tribal: Every morning another family waits at {Ns} hearth with a quarrel about a share of meat or a broken promise, and every evening {partner} eats alone.
scene magic: Petitions come under {Ns} door every morning, some sealed with a guild's wax, some scrawled on scraps, and {Ns} own workbench gathers dust.
- W | answer every case in turn, oldest first, however late the nights run | habit | v: conformity, benevolence | self_control: + | tribal: hear every quarrel in turn, oldest first, however late the fire burns | magic: answer every petition in turn, oldest first, however late the candles burn | chance: 65
- U | sort the cases into the few problems behind them, and take each problem to the council | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: find the few grievances behind all the quarrels, and bring each one to the fire | magic: sort the petitions into the few wrongs behind them, and take each one to the council | chance: 45
- B | take on the cases that will be noticed, and pass the rest to the officers | v: power, achievement | tribal: take up the quarrels the whole band will hear of, and send the rest to the elders | magic: take up the petitions the town will hear of, and pass the rest to the clerks | chance: 92
- R | drop everything for the family in the damp flat, and go round tonight | v: benevolence, stimulation | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: drop everything for the family whose shelter leaks, and go to them tonight | magic: drop everything for the family in the damp garret, and go round tonight | chance: 92
- G | keep one evening a week for your own family, and let the ward wait | v: tradition, benevolence | self_control: + | tribal: keep one evening in every few for your own kin, and let the band wait | chance: 72
- W>B | set up a fixed surgery every Saturday, and tell residents to bring their cases there | v: security, conformity | tribal: name one day each moon when families may bring their quarrels to your hearth | magic: set one fixed day a week for petitions, and turn the rest away | chance: 90
- U>R | set up a simple online form so cases arrive with the facts, and free your evenings | v: self-direction, hedonism | tribal: teach the families to bring their witnesses with them, and free your evenings | magic: have a clerk take every petition down in a set form, and free your evenings | chance: 72
- B>G | get a friend in the housing office to move your ward's cases up the list | v: power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: jumping one ward's residents up a shared waiting list; backfire: another councillor finds out, and the standards committee asks questions | self_control: - | tribal: get a friend among the elders to settle your own hearths' quarrels first | magic: get a friend among the clerks to move your ward's petitions to the top | chance: 85
- R>W | read out the month's worst case in the chamber, again and again, until the council acts | v: universalism, stimulation | tribal: speak of the worst wrong at every fire until the elders act | magic: read the worst petition aloud at every sitting until the council acts | chance: 60
- G>U | ask the old caseworker at the community centre how she handled these streets for years | v: tradition, achievement | grants: constituency casework | tribal: ask the old woman who settled the band's quarrels for years how she did it | magic: ask the old petition-writer of the ward how she handled these lanes for years | chance: 72
works: By the end of the month the cases have a shape, and {N} still has evenings at home.
works: A case that has dragged on for a year is settled, and the family stops {N} in the street to say thanks.
fails: The cases keep coming faster than {N} can answer, and the ward starts to say {N} never replies.
fails: {partner} says one quiet evening that it feels as if {N} married the ward.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 74. step 2, transform (cycle s3)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 3 options (check.py section 4).
== whose councillor you are
tier: inner
threshold: title:local councillor
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
times: per new local councillor: once in the threshold season, for about half of new councillors (the season's middle step brings this moment or 'the casework that never stops'); about 1 life in 100 sits on a council (catalogue share), so about 1 in 200 meet it
holds: local councillor
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: public life, meaning
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague, friend
earth: ten weeks in, a vote on closing the ward's library, and the party, the cause, the ward and your own ambition each want something different
tribal: two moons in, a choice at the fire about moving camp, and the faction, your own hearths, the young hunters who backed you and your own hopes each pull another way
magic: ten weeks in, a vote on selling the ward's old reading room, and the faction, the guild that paid for your banners, the lane and your own hopes each want something different
requires: in the threshold season of local councillor, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, when the first vote comes on which the party, the cause and the ward disagree
likelier: a councillor who stood for a cause as well as a party; a ward that votes differently from the town; a leader who keeps a tight whip
rarer: an independent with no group to answer to; a council with no contested votes that season
scene: A vote is coming on closing the library in {Ns} ward to save money for the whole town. The group has a line, the people who campaigned for {N} have another, the ward has a third, and {boss} says ambitious councillors do not make trouble in their first year.
scene W: {N} thinks of the declaration {N} signed: to serve the whole town, fairly and by the rules, not just the streets that voted.
scene U: {N} has read the library's figures, the visitor counts and the cost of every alternative, and thinks the answer is in the numbers, if anyone will look.
scene B: A seat on the council is a first rung. {N} can see the ladder from here: a committee chair, the cabinet, a parliamentary selection.
scene R: The people who knocked on doors in the rain for {N} did it for the cause. {N} cannot look them in the eye and vote the way the group wants.
scene G: The library is where {N} learned to read. Whatever the town needs, the ward is home.
scene tribal: The band must choose whether to move camp to the far shore. The faction's heads want one thing, {Ns} own hearths another, and the young hunters who backed {N} a third.
scene magic: The council will vote on selling the ward's old reading room. The faction wants it sold, the guild that paid for {Ns} banners wants it kept, and the lane is waiting to see.
- W | serve the whole town by the rules, even when your own ward loses | identity | binds | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: speak for the whole band by its customs, even when your own hearths lose | chance: 72
- U | decide every vote on the evidence, and publish your reasons each time | identity | habit | v: universalism, self-direction | tribal: weigh every choice on what is known, and give your reasons at the fire each time | chance: 75
- B | treat the seat as the first rung, and vote with the group's leaders | identity | v: achievement, power | tribal: treat your voice as the first step, and stand with the faction's heads | magic: treat the seat as the first rung, and cast with the faction's lords | chance: 90
- R | vote with the cause you stood for, whatever the group decides | identity | binds | v: stimulation, universalism | mark: defied an authority | tribal: stand with the young hunters who backed you, whatever the faction decides | magic: cast with the cause you stood for, whatever the faction decides | chance: 70
- G | put the ward first in every vote, as one of its own | identity | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: put your own hearths first in every choice, as one of them | magic: put the lane first in every vote, as one of its own | chance: 76
- W>R | take the town's hardest unsolved case as your cause, and see it through by the book | identity | v: universalism, stimulation | tribal: take up the band's oldest unsettled wrong, and see it through by custom | chance: 40
- U>G | spend your term on one long plan for the ward, worked out with its residents | identity | binds | v: universalism, tradition | tribal: work out one long plan for your hearths' future with the families themselves | chance: 62
- B>W | build a bloc of councillors from both sides to run the committees properly | identity | binds | v: power | grants: coalition building | tribal: bind voices from both factions together to keep the fire's business in order | magic: bind councillors from both factions together to run the committees properly | chance: 45
- R>U | push the council to try what no council has tried, and learn as you go | identity | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: push the band to try a way nobody has tried, and learn as it goes | chance: 35
- G>B | become the ward's fixer, the one who knows whom to call to get things done | identity | v: power | tribal: become the one who settles things quietly between the families of your hearths | magic: become the lane's fixer, the one who knows whom to ask at the guild hall | chance: 48
works: The vote comes, {N} votes as {N} decided to, and for once the seat feels like {Ns} own.
works: Within weeks people in the ward can say what kind of councillor {N} is, and most of them say it with respect.
fails: Whichever way {N} turns, someone who trusted {N} feels betrayed, and says so.
fails: {N} tries to be everyone's councillor and ends the month as nobody's.

