scene: Five months in, the budget goes to the vote. There is a gap of a few percent between what the town has and what it has been promised, and every line {N} cuts is someone's job, someone's bus route or someone's library.
scene W: The law says the budget must balance. {N} has the clerk's note on that pinned to the wall.
scene U: {N} knows every line now. The gap could be closed four ways, and {N} has costed all four.
scene B: Every councillor who wants something in the budget owes {N} a vote for it. {N} is counting.
scene R: {N} hates this part: choosing who loses. Some nights {N} wants to throw the whole spreadsheet out of the window.
scene G: {N} thinks of the old swimming baths, the allotments, the band in the park: the things that make the town itself, which a spreadsheet does not count.
scene tribal: Winter is coming, and {N} must share out the stores. There is not enough for every family to have what it had last year, and each share {N} names will be remembered.
scene magic: The coffer is short, every guild sends a petition for more, and the council will vote on {Ns} first budget at the next sitting.
