scene: On {Ns} first day in parliament {N} swears the oath, is given a pass, a locker and a desk in an office shared with three other new members, and finds the chamber so full there is nowhere to sit. Somebody shows {N} the way to the tea room twice.
scene W: {N} reads the oath slowly and feels it settle: the law, the house, the people of the constituency. It is not nothing.
scene U: The building is a maze of corridors, rules and customs older than the country's roads. {N} has a map, a rule book and a notebook, and is filling the notebook fast.
scene B: Old hands know within a week which new members to watch. {N} means to be on that list.
scene R: Under the painted ceiling {N} feels like a child let into the grown-ups' party, and wants to stand up and cheer.
scene G: {N} thinks of the town back home: the bus stop where the campaign started, and the people who will be watching the news tonight to catch a glimpse.
scene tribal: At the great gathering {N} walks among hundreds of strangers and their fires as the speaker for {Ns} band, and does not yet know where to sit.
scene magic: Under the Order's seal {N} swears the oath of the Assembly, is given a pass warded to {Ns} own hand, and finds no room on the crowded benches.
