== who you answer to
tier: inner
threshold: title:member of parliament
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
times: per new member of parliament: once in the threshold season, for about half of new members (the season's middle step brings this moment or 'the maiden speech'); about 1 life in 10,000 sits in parliament (House of Commons, ONS), so about 1 in 20,000 meet it
holds: member of parliament
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: public life, meaning
horizon: years
roles: boss, partner, colleague
earth: ten weeks in, the whips, the constituency, your conscience and your ambition all want your vote on the same bill, and you must decide whom you answer to
tribal: two moons in, at the gathering, the faction's head, the families back home, your own judgment and your hunger to rise all pull at your voice
magic: ten weeks in, the faction's warden, the city that sent you, your conscience and your hopes at court all want your lot cast their way
requires: in the threshold season of member of parliament, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, when the first whipped vote comes on which the party, the constituency and the member's own view disagree
likelier: a party in government with a big programme; a constituency with its own interest in the bill; a member elected on a local cause
rarer: an independent with no whip; a parliament in recess for most of the season
scene: Ten weeks in, a bill comes to its second reading. The whips want {Ns} vote, the constituency is against it, {Ns} own reading of it is mixed, and a career in the house may turn on the answer. On the phone that night {partner} asks whom {N} works for now.
scene W: {N} was elected on the party's promises and swore an oath to the house. Both say the same thing: keep faith with the institution that sent {N}.
scene U: {N} has read the bill, the briefings from both sides and the research behind them. The evidence points one way, and nobody else seems to have read it.
scene B: The whips keep lists. A loyal vote now is a post in two years; a rebel is a backbencher for a decade.
scene R: {N} can feel in the gut what is right, and it is not what the whips want.
scene G: {N} thinks of the town: the factory gate, the chapel, the families who have sent someone like {N} to this place for three generations.
scene tribal: At the gathering the faction's head wants {Ns} voice for a raid, {Ns} own band wants peace, and {N} can feel which way the chief's favour lies.
scene magic: The faction's warden brings the line for tomorrow's vote in the Assembly. {Ns} city has sent a letter against it, and {Ns} hopes at court lie with the faction.
- W | vote with the party you were elected for, and keep faith with its promises | identity | v: conformity, security | tribal: stand with the faction that sent you, and keep faith with its word | magic: cast with the faction whose seal you carry, and keep faith with its promises | chance: 82
- U | read every bill yourself, and vote on the evidence whatever the whips say | identity | v: universalism, self-direction | tribal: weigh every choice yourself, and speak on what you know, whatever the faction says | magic: read every decree yourself, and cast on the evidence, whatever the wardens say | chance: 74
- B | vote with the whips, and make sure they know it, for a post later | identity | v: power, achievement | tribal: stand with the faction's heads, and make sure they remember it when duties are given | magic: cast with the faction, and make sure its wardens remember it when offices are given | chance: 91
- R | vote with your conscience against the whip, and say why in the chamber | identity | binds | v: stimulation, universalism | mark: defied an authority | self_control: + | takes_if_fails: the party whip | tribal: stand against the faction when your heart says so, and say why at the fire | magic: cast against the faction's line when your conscience says so, and say why in the Assembly | chance: 68
- G | vote as the constituency wants, every time, as one of its own | identity | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: speak as your band wants, every time, as one of its own | magic: cast as your city wants, every time, as one of its own | chance: 72
- W>R | promise the constituency in public to vote against any bill that hurts it | identity | binds | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: kept your word | tribal: swear to your band at the fire to oppose anything that hurts it | magic: swear to your city in its square to oppose any decree that hurts it | chance: 45
- U>G | become the house's expert on the one thing the constituency lives by | identity | binds | v: achievement, tradition | tribal: become the gathering's best knower of the one thing your band lives by | magic: become the Assembly's master of the one trade your city lives by | chance: 72
- B>W | trade your vote for money for the constituency, promised in writing | identity | binds | v: power, benevolence | grants: counting the votes | tribal: trade your voice for a share of the hunting grounds for your band, sworn before the elders | magic: trade your lot for coin for your city, promised under seal | chance: 35
- R>U | join the rebels who want the bill rewritten, and help draft the amendment | identity | v: stimulation, universalism | grants: drafting policy | tribal: join the speakers who want the choice remade, and help shape the new words | magic: join the rebels who want the decree rewritten, and help draft the new clauses | chance: 71
- G>B | build your own power base in the region, so no whip can ever touch you | identity | v: power | tribal: gather the bands of your own valley behind you, so no faction head can touch you | magic: gather the towns of your own province behind you, so no warden can touch you | chance: 30
works: The vote is cast, and {N} can explain it to anyone, at home or in the house, without looking away.
works: Over the following weeks the people who matter to {N} come to see what kind of member {N} is, and most of them respect it.
fails: Whichever way {N} goes, someone feels betrayed: the whips, the people back home, or {Ns} own conscience.
fails: {N} tries to please every side and ends up trusted by none of them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 83. step 3 (cycle s4)

== the Thursday train home
tier: inner
threshold: title:member of parliament
step: 3
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
times: per new member of parliament: once, about five months after taking the title, near the end of the threshold season; about 1 life in 10,000 (House of Commons, ONS)
holds: member of parliament
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: public life, family, friends
horizon: months
roles: partner, friend, colleague
earth: five months in, the Thursday train home: the weekend surgery, a partner who has run the house alone all week, and old friends who now want favours
tribal: five moons in, the gathering rests and you walk home to your own hearth, where your family has managed without you and old friends want your voice
magic: five months in, the Assembly rises for the week and the coach takes you home to a household run without you, and to old friends with petitions
requires: in the threshold season of member of parliament, within the first six months after taking the title: step 3, near the end of the six months, as the weeks in the capital and the weekends at home settle into a pattern
likelier: a constituency far from the capital; a partner and family who stayed at home; a member who kept old friends in the town
rarer: a seat in the capital itself; a member who lives alone or moved the family to the capital
scene: Five months in, {N} knows the Thursday train by heart: the same seat, the case of constituency letters, the window going dark. At home {partner} has run the house alone since Monday, and the weekend is already full: a surgery on Friday, a fete on Saturday, and an old friend who wants a quick word about a planning application.
scene W: {N} has kept every promise to the constituency and very few to the family. On the train {N} writes a list of both.
scene U: {N} uses the three hours on the train to work through the week's casework, and has not looked out of the window in months.
scene B: The old friend's quick word is about a planning application. {N} knows exactly what is being asked, and what it might be worth.
scene R: {N} misses the old life: Friday nights out, saying whatever came to mind, and nobody watching.
scene G: The town comes into view: the church tower, the river, the lights of the estate. Home, and it feels further away each week.
scene tribal: The gathering rests for a moon, and {N} walks home to {Ns} own hearth, where {partner} has kept everything going alone and old friends are already waiting with favours to ask.
scene magic: The Assembly rises for the week, and the coach takes {N} home to a household run without {N}, and to old friends with petitions in hand.
- W | keep Sunday for the family, whoever calls, and tell the constituency so | v: benevolence, security | tribal: keep one day at home for your kin, whoever comes to your hearth | magic: keep the rest-day for your household, whoever knocks, and tell the city so | chance: 74
- U | plan the weeks to the hour so the family gets fixed time, written in the diary | v: achievement, security | tribal: plan the moons ahead so your kin know when you will be home | magic: plan the weeks to the hour so your household gets fixed time, written in the book | chance: 88
- B | promise the old friend a look at the application, and make sure they owe you | v: power | tribal: promise the old friend your voice, and make sure they owe you | magic: promise the old friend a look at the petition, and make sure they owe you | chance: 94
- R | skip the Saturday fete and take your partner away for the weekend | door | v: hedonism, benevolence | tribal: leave the feast early and take your partner up into the hills for a few days | magic: skip the Saturday fair and take your partner away to the coast | chance: 95
- G | spend Saturday morning at the allotment on your own street, as you always did | v: tradition, hedonism | tribal: spend a morning gathering at the old places, as you always did | chance: 85
- W>G | bring the family along to the Friday surgery, so the work becomes a family thing | v: benevolence | tribal: bring your kin to sit with you when the families come with their troubles | magic: bring your household to the petition day, so the work becomes a family thing | chance: 52
- U>W | write down what you can and cannot do for friends who ask, and share it | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: tell your friends plainly at the fire what you can and cannot do for them | magic: write out what you can and cannot do for friends who ask, and send it round | chance: 85
- B>U | hire a sharp office manager to run the weekends, and buy back your Saturdays | v: achievement | tribal: give a sharp young kinsman the families' troubles to sort, and win back your days | magic: hire a sharp steward to run the petition days, and buy back your rest-day | chance: 72
- R>B | turn the fete into a rally: speak from a lorry and work the crowd | v: stimulation, power | grants: rousing a crowd | tribal: turn the feast into a rally: climb the rock and stir the crowd | magic: turn the fair into a rally: speak from a cart and work the crowd | chance: 89
- G>R | walk the dog by the river on Sunday morning, phone switched off | habit | v: hedonism, self-direction | tribal: walk alone by the river at dawn, with no one to answer | magic: walk by the river on the rest-day morning, with no one to answer | chance: 95
works: The weekend holds, and on the last evening {N} leaves feeling like a person, not only a member.
works: {partner} says that, for the first time in months, it felt as if {N} had come home.
fails: The calls and the callers keep coming all weekend, and Sunday is gone before it starts.
fails: {N} leaves again with the feeling that home has learned to run without {N}.

# ================================================================ minister (ages 21-95)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 84. step 1 (cycle s1)

== the first morning at the department
tier: inner
threshold: title:minister
step: 1
stages: adult mature elder
age: 21-95
times: per new minister: once, in the first weeks after taking the title; about 1 life in 30,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: minister
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: public life, work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, friend
earth: the first morning as a minister: a car at the door, a private office of strangers, a box of papers and a department of thousands
tribal: the first morning as keeper of one of the chief's duties, the hunt's share, the peace or the trade, with the old hands who carried it for the last keeper
magic: the first morning in an office of the Crown: the seal on the desk, ledgers to the ceiling, and clerks who served ten councillors before you
requires: in the threshold season of minister, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'the phone call from the leader' or any other way in): step 1, the first weeks in the department
likelier: a first ministerial post; a new government filling every post at once; a department the person has never worked in
rarer: a minister moved sideways from another department; a post without a department of its own
scene: On the first morning a car comes for {N}. At the department a private office of six strangers stands up when {N} walks in, the permanent secretary shakes hands, and there is a box of papers on the desk with a note: "Decisions needed by Friday."
scene W: {N} reads the code for ministers and the rules on gifts and interests before opening the box. It seems the right order.
scene U: There are two thousand pages of briefing. {N} reads the summaries first, then the annexes, and starts a list of questions.
scene B: The department has a budget larger than some countries. {N} looks at the organisation chart and sees where the power sits.
scene R: {N} grins at the car, the office and the people standing up, and wants to do something on day one that the country will notice.
scene G: On the wall is a list of every minister since the department began. Some of the names {N} learned at school.
scene tribal: The chief has given {N} the keeping of one duty: the hunt's share, the peace, or the trade with other clans. The old hands who kept it for the last keeper wait to see what {N} is.
scene magic: On the desk in the office of the Crown lies a seal, and beside it ledgers to the ceiling. The clerks, who served ten councillors before {N}, bow and wait.
- W | read the code for ministers, and declare every interest before opening the box | v: conformity, security | tribal: ask the elders which gifts a keeper of the chief's duty may take, before taking any | magic: read the code of the Crown's councillors, and declare every interest before breaking a seal | chance: 96
- U | read the whole briefing, and send back a list of hard questions by evening | v: achievement | tribal: hear out every old hand who kept the duty before you, and come back with hard questions | magic: read the clerks' briefing rolls end to end, and send back hard questions by evening | chance: 88
- B | name your own adviser on the first day, before the department offers one | v: power | tribal: choose your own right hand on the first day, before the old hands offer one | magic: name your own secretary on the first day, before the clerks offer one | chance: 85
- R | announce one change on the first day, on the steps, before anyone can stop you | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: change one thing about the duty on the first day, before anyone can stop you | magic: proclaim one change on the first day, from the steps, before anyone can stop you | chance: 35
- G | walk the whole building on the first morning, floor by floor, and meet the staff | body: light | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | tribal: walk out to every camp the duty touches, and meet the people who carry it | magic: climb the whole tower of offices on the first morning, and meet every clerk | chance: 85
- W>U | ask the permanent secretary for a frank account of what went wrong last time | door | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: ask the old hands for a plain account of where the last keeper went wrong | magic: ask the chief clerk for a frank account of where your predecessor went wrong | chance: 83
- U>B | work out which three decisions in the box will shape the year, and take them yourself | v: power, achievement | tribal: work out which three choices will shape the year, and keep them for yourself | magic: work out which three seals will shape the year, and set them yourself | chance: 68
- B>R | send the car away and take the bus, and make sure the cameras see it | v: hedonism, power | tribal: refuse the escort of young hunters and walk to the chief's fire alone, and let everyone see | magic: send the carriage away and walk through the market, and make sure the heralds see it | chance: 94
- R>G | ring the friends from home who were there at the start, and tell them first | v: benevolence | tribal: send word first to the friends at your own hearth who were there at the start | magic: write first to the friends at home who were there at the start | chance: 97
- G>W | keep the private office your predecessor left, and trust the staff who know the work | v: tradition, security | tribal: keep the old hands the last keeper left, and trust them to know the work | magic: keep the clerks your predecessor left, and trust them to know the work | chance: 68
works: By the end of the first day {N} has a desk, a team and the first decisions made.
works: The people around {N} go home that evening saying the new minister will be all right.
fails: The first days are swallowed by introductions and rules, and the pile of decisions is still waiting at the end of the week.
fails: Something said on the first morning travels round the whole place by noon, and not in {Ns} favour.

