rarer: a leader who takes over in mid-term and keeps the same cabinet; a caretaker head of government
scene: The cameras flash, {N} says a few words on the step, and the door closes behind {N}. Inside, the staff line the corridor and clap. Then the cabinet secretary takes {N} to a small room with a pile of sealed letters, briefings only the head of government is told, and a file the last government hoped would never come out.
scene W: {N} reads the guidance on the constitution and the office on the first night, slowly, the way some people read scripture.
scene U: The secret briefings are worse and stranger than {N} imagined. {N} has questions, and the first night is not long enough for them.
scene B: The whole machinery of the state now answers to {N}. {N} can feel it hum, and sets out to learn which levers move what.
scene R: {N} can hardly believe it, and wants to ring {friend}, laugh out loud and open a window to the noise outside.
scene G: In the corridor hang the portraits of everyone who held the office before. {N} looks at the oldest one for a long time.
scene tribal: As the great fire is lit, the heads of the clans sit down around it and turn to {N}. {N} has seen chiefs sit in that place since childhood, and now it is {Ns}.
scene magic: At the foot of the throne {N} kneels and receives the Chancellor's chain. Then the doors close, and the clerks lead {N} up a tower of rooms full of strangers and sealed letters.
